<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
  <section xml:id="getting-started-first-3d-game-01-game-setup-setting-up-the-game-area">
    <title>Setting up the game area</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>In this first part, we're going to set up the game area. Let's get started by
            importing the start assets and setting up the game scene.</p>
    <p>We've prepared a Godot project with the 3D models and sounds we'll use for this
            tutorial, linked in the index page. If you haven't done so yet, you can download
            the archive here: <url href="https://github.com/godotengine/godot-docs-project-starters/releases/download/latest-4.x/3d_squash_the_creeps_starter.zip">Squash the Creeps assets</url>.</p>
    <p>Once you've downloaded it, extract the .zip archive on your computer. Open the
            Godot Project Manager and click the <em>Import</em> button.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Import Project Button</caption>
      <image source="01.import_button.webp" >
         <shortdescription>An empty project manager window with import surrounded by a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>In the import popup, enter the full path to the freshly created directory
            <c>3d_squash_the_creeps_starter/</c>. You can click the <em>Browse</em> button on the right to
            open a file browser and navigate to the <c>project.godot</c> file the folder
            contains.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Browse and Select Project Window</caption>
      <image source="02.browse_to_project_folder.webp" >
         <shortdescription>project.godot file is surrounded by a yellow box in the 3d_squash_the_creeps_starter folder.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>Click <em>Import</em> to open the project in the editor.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Import Project Dialog</caption>
      <image source="03.import_and_edit.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The import button is surrounded by a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>A window notifying you that the project was generated by an older Godot version may appear.
            Click <em>OK</em> to convert the project to your current Godot version.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Upgrade Project Dialog</caption>
      <image source="import_project_to_4.x_prompt.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The OK button is surrounded by a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>If it doesn't open immediately, open the project from your project list.</p>
    <p>The start project contains an icon and two folders: <c>art/</c> and <c>fonts/</c>.
            There, you will find the art assets and music we'll use in the game.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Filesystem View of Assets</caption>
      <image source="04.start_assets.webp" >
         <shortdescription>Art, fonts, and a custom icon are shown in the FileSystem Dock</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>There are two 3D models, <c>player.glb</c> and <c>mob.glb</c>, some materials that
            belong to these models, and a music track.</p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-first-3d-game-01-game-setup-setting-up-the-playable-area">
      <title>Setting up the playable area</title>
      <p>We're going to create our main scene with a plain <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_node.html#class-node">Node</url> as its root. In the
                <em>Scene</em> dock, click the <em>Add Child Node</em> button represented by a "+" icon in the
                top-left and double-click on <em>Node</em>. Name the node <c>Main</c>. An alternate method to rename the node is to right-click on <em>Node</em> and choose <em>Rename</em> (or <c>F2</c>). Alternatively, to add
                a node to the scene, you can press <c>Ctrl</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>A</c> (<c>Cmd</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>A</c> on macOS).</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Create Main Node</caption>
      <image source="05.main_node.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The Main Node is alone in the Scene Dock.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Save the scene as <c>main.tscn</c> by pressing <c>Ctrl</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>S</c> (<c>Cmd</c>
        <c />+<c />
        <c>S</c> on macOS).</p>
      <p>We'll start by adding a floor that'll prevent the characters from falling. To
                create static colliders like the floor, walls, or ceilings, you can use <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_staticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d">StaticBody3D</url> nodes. They require <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_collisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d">CollisionShape3D</url> child nodes to
                define the collision area. With the <c>Main</c> node selected, add a <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_staticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d">StaticBody3D</url>
                node, then a <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_collisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d">CollisionShape3D</url>. Rename the <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_staticbody3d.html#class-staticbody3d">StaticBody3D</url> to <c>Ground</c>.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Add a Static Body 3D</caption>
      <image source="adding_static_body3D.webp" >
         <shortdescription>StaticBody3D is selected in the Create New Node Dialog.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Your scene tree should look like this</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Scene Tree</caption>
      <image source="06.staticbody_node.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The scene dock is shown with the Ground as a child of main and a CollisionShape3D as the child of Ground.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>A warning sign next to the <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_collisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d">CollisionShape3D</url> appears because we haven't defined
                its shape. If you click the icon, a popup appears to give you more information.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Collision Shape Warning Dialog</caption>
      <image source="07.collision_shape_warning.webp" >
         <shortdescription>A dialog warning to make a shape resource for the CollisionShape3D.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>To create a shape, select the <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_collisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d">CollisionShape3D</url> node, head to the <em>Inspector</em>
                and click the <em>&lt;empty&gt;</em> field next to the <em>Shape</em> property. Create a new <em>BoxShape3D</em>.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Adding Collision Shape in Inspector</caption>
      <image source="08.create_box_shape3D.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The Inspector Dock is shown with the shape selection menu and BoxShape3D surrounded with a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>The box shape is perfect for flat ground and walls. Its thickness makes it
                reliable to block even fast-moving objects.</p>
      <p>A box's wireframe appears in the viewport with three orange dots. You can click
                and drag these to edit the shape's extents interactively. We can also precisely
                set the size in the inspector. Click on the <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_boxshape3d.html#class-boxshape3d">BoxShape3D</url> to expand the resource.
                Set its <em>Size</em> to <c>60</c> on the X-axis, <c>2</c> for the Y-axis, and <c>60</c> for
                the Z-axis.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Setting the Size of the Box</caption>
      <image source="09.box_size.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The size of the BoxShape3D is shown in the inspector with x set to 60, y set to 2 and z set to 60.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Collision shapes are invisible. We need to add a visual floor that goes along
                with it. Select the <c>Ground</c> node and add a <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_meshinstance3d.html#class-meshinstance3d">MeshInstance3D</url> as its child.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Adding A MeshInstance3D</caption>
      <image source="10.mesh_instance3d.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The scene is shown with a MeshInstance3D added as a child node of Ground.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>In the <em>Inspector</em>, click on the field next to <em>Mesh</em> and create a <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_boxmesh.html#class-boxmesh">BoxMesh</url>
                resource to create a visible box.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Adding Box Mesh in Inspector</caption>
      <image source="11.box_mesh.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The Inspector is shown with a mesh menu selected and the BoxMesh item surrounded with a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Once again, it's too small by default. Click the box icon to expand the
                resource and set its <em>Size</em> to <c>60</c>, <c>2</c>, and <c>60</c>.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Resizing the Mesh</caption>
      <image source="12.cube_resized.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The size of the Mesh is set to 60,2,60.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>You should see a wide grey slab that covers the grid and blue and red axes in
                the viewport.</p>
      <p>We're going to move the ground down so we can see the floor grid. To do this, the grid snapping feature can be used.
                Grid snapping can be activated 2 ways in the 3D editor.
                The first is by pressing the <em>Use Snap</em> button (or pressing the <c>Y</c> key).
                The second is by selecting a node, dragging a handle on the gizmo <term>then</term> holding <c>Ctrl</c> (<c>Cmd</c> on macOS) while holding the selected node.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Grid Snapping Button</caption>
      <image source="use_snap.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The grid snapping button is surrounded by a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Start by setting snapping with your preferred method. Then move the <c>Ground</c> node using the Y-axis (the green arrow on the gizmo).</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Move Gizmo Y-Axis</caption>
      <image source="move_gizmo_y_axis.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The gizmo axis is shown in the center of the 3D viewport.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>If you can't see the 3D object manipulator like on the image above, ensure
                    the <em>Transform Mode</em> is active in the toolbar above the view.</p>
        </note>
      <figure>
        <caption>Select Transform Mode</caption>
      <image source="14.select_mode_icon.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The select transform Mode button is surrounded by a yellow box.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Move the ground down <c>1</c> meter, in order to have a visible editor grid. A label in the bottom-left corner of the
                viewport tells you how much you're translating the node.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Translating Label</caption>
      <image source="15.translation_amount.png" >
         <shortdescription>The image reads Translating: (0,-1,0).</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <note>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>Moving the <em>Ground</em> node down moves both children along with it.
                    Ensure you move the <em>Ground</em> node, <term>not</term> the <em>MeshInstance3D</em> or the
                    <em>CollisionShape3D</em>.</p>
        </note>
      <p>Ultimately, <c>Ground</c>'s transform.position.y should be -1</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Transforming the Ground Level</caption>
      <image source="ground_down1meter.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The inspector shows the Node3D y position set -1.0 with a yellow box around it.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>Let's add a directional light so our scene isn't all grey. Select the <c>Main</c>
                node and add a child node <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_directionallight3d.html#class-directionallight3d">DirectionalLight3D</url>.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Create Directional Light</caption>
      <image source="create_directional_light3d.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The Create New Node dialog shows the DirectionalLight3D node selected.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>We need to move and rotate the <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_directionallight3d.html#class-directionallight3d">DirectionalLight3D</url> node.
                Move it up by clicking and dragging on the manipulator's green arrow
                and click and drag on the red arc to rotate it around the X-axis, until the
                ground is lit.</p>
      <p>In the <em>Inspector</em>, turn on <em>Shadow</em> by clicking the checkbox.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Toggle Shadows</caption>
      <image source="16.turn_on_shadows.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The inspector dock is shown with the Light3D shadow on checkbox selected.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>At this point, your project should look like this.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Project View with Light</caption>
      <image source="17.project_with_light.webp" >
         <shortdescription>The 3D viewport shows a white square ground with a light above it in the center.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      <p>That's our starting point. In the next part, we will work on the player scene
                and base movement.</p>
      </subsection>
    </section>
